  "articleBody": "Choosing paint for your bathroom involves balancing finish appearance, washability, and long-term moisture defense. Homeowners often wonder whether standard interior latex is adequate or if paying for specialized bathroom paint is truly necessary.\n\nThe practical difference lies in the resin formulation and biocides. Standard flat or eggshell latex absorbs airborne steam, which causes the film to soften, stain, and peel in enclosed shower rooms. Specialized bathroom paints contain mildewcides and tight acrylic binders that resist humidity, frequent scrubbing, and moisture penetration, even in lower-sheen finishes like matte or satin. While standard gloss or semi-gloss paint sheds water, it highlights every wall imperfection, nail pop, and uneven drywall patch.\n\nHow to Compare Your Options:\nIn small, poorly ventilated powder rooms, a quality standard satin paint over a good primer is often sufficient and more economical. However, for full bathrooms with daily shower steam, investing in a moisture-resistant coating designed specifically for damp spaces prevents repeat repaints and water-streaked walls. If you have active moisture leaks or peeling drywall, remember that paint alone will never fix structural water problems. When you want clean lines, drywall repair, and expert coating application across Aurora and Denver, contact Abraham Becerra at HH Remodeling LLC at 303-505-0866.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
